# D4 · Image Contrast Examples

**Part:** D – Contrast · **Reading time:** ~40 min · **Pass 1 overview:** [P1.4 Contrast and Image Quality](../pass1_big_picture/P1.4_Contrast_and_Image_Quality.ipynb) · **Refreshers you may want:** [RM6 2D Fourier and images](../refreshers/RM6_2D_Fourier_and_Images.ipynb), [RM5 Fourier pairs and sampling](../refreshers/RM5_Fourier_Pairs_and_Sampling.ipynb)

**Why this chapter matters.** The previous chapters ([D1](../pass2_deep_dive/D1_Basic_Contrast_Mechanisms.ipynb), [D2](../pass2_deep_dive/D2_In_Vivo_Contrast_Mechanisms.ipynb), [D3](../pass2_deep_dive/D3_Gradient_and_Spin_Echoes.ipynb)) gave the formulas. Here you practise the reverse direction, which is what you do in front of a real image: *look at the brightness of known tissues and infer how the image was made*, and *measure signals and infer the tissue parameters*. We use real example images, a simple simulated phantom (port of the original code) and a "read the contrast" exercise.

<<<md>>>
## Learning goals

1. Describe how various types of MRI contrast are created
    * Identify various image contrasts
2. *(added)* Infer the weighting (T1w, T2w, PDw, FLAIR, STIR, post-contrast) of an image from the relative brightness of reference tissues
3. *(added)* Estimate $T_1$ and $T_2$ from images acquired with different TR and TE, and with two flip angles (variable flip angle method)

<<<md>>>
## Symbols used in this chapter

| Symbol | Meaning (plain words) | SI unit | Typical value / remark |
|---|---|---|---|
| $S$ | image signal (pixel value, magnitude) | a.u. | |
| $M_0$ | equilibrium magnetization ∝ proton density | normalized | |
| $T_1$, $T_2$, $T_2^*$ | relaxation time constants | s | |
| $TE$, $TR$, $TI$ | echo, repetition, inversion time | s | |
| $\theta$ | flip angle | rad (°) | |
| $E_1=e^{-TR/T_1}$ | shorthand | – | |
| $m(\vec r)$ | image (transverse magnetization at TE) | a.u. | |
| $M(k_x,k_y)$ | k-space data (**not** magnetization here) | a.u. | |
| $k_x,k_y$, $k_r=\sqrt{k_x^2+k_y^2}$ | spatial frequency; radial spatial frequency | m⁻¹ (here: cycles/pixel) | |
| $\mathrm{circ}(r)$, $\mathrm{jinc}(x)=J_1(\pi x)/(\pi x)$ | disk function; its Fourier transform's shape | – | $J_1$: Bessel function of the first kind, order 1 |
| $R$ | disk radius | m (here: fraction of FOV) | |
| $(x_c,y_c)$ | disk centre | fraction of FOV | |
| $\sigma_n$ | noise standard deviation | a.u. | |
| $C$ | contrast-agent concentration | mM | |

<<<code>>>
import sys, os
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.abspath(".."))   # make ../mri_tools.py importable
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import mri_tools as mt
mt.setup_plots()
%matplotlib inline

<<<md>>>
## 1. Real image contrast examples

A practical reading recipe – check the **reference tissues** first:

| tissue | T1w | T2w | PDw | FLAIR | STIR | T1w + Gd |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| CSF / free fluid | dark | **bright** | grey (≈ tissue) | **dark** | bright | dark |
| WM vs GM (brain) | WM > GM | GM > WM | GM ≳ WM (low contrast) | GM > WM | GM > WM | WM > GM |
| fat (scalp, marrow) | bright | bright on FSE, intermediate on SE | bright | bright-ish | **dark** | bright (unless fat-sat) |
| edema / most lesions | dark | bright | bright | **bright** | bright | enhancing if blood–brain barrier broken |
| vessels with flow | dark (flow void) on SE | dark (flow void) | dark | dark | dark | bright (enhanced) |

### Example 1 – brain (from Pauly and Gold)

![Brain contrast examples (from Pauly and Gold): top row three different weightings of the same slice, bottom row a slice before and after contrast](../images/contrast_brain_example.png)

**Exercise:** name the weighting of each of the five images before opening the answer.

<details><summary>Answer (our reading – acquisition parameters are not given in the source)</summary>

* **Top left – PD-weighted:** brain bright overall, GM slightly brighter than WM, CSF in the ventricles not dark and not much brighter than tissue, low overall contrast.
* **Top middle – $T_1$-weighted:** CSF dark, WM slightly brighter than GM, vessels dark.
* **Top right – $T_2$-weighted:** CSF very bright, GM brighter than WM.
* **Bottom left – $T_1$-weighted, pre-contrast:** subtle abnormality in the right hemisphere (left side of the image).
* **Bottom right – $T_1$-weighted after gadolinium:** a strongly enhancing (bright) lesion – contrast agent has leaked through a broken blood–brain barrier and shortened $T_1$ ([D2](../pass2_deep_dive/D2_In_Vivo_Contrast_Mechanisms.ipynb)).

</details>

